.TH HIRZ\-NMH 1 2026-11-16 hirz "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hirz\-nmh \- native messaging host of the hirz Firefox extension
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hirz\-nmh
.RI [ MANIFEST " [" EXTENSION\-ID ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hirz\-nmh
is started by Firefox when the hirz extension (id
.BR hirz@berkati.xyz )
connects to the native application
.BR hirz .
It is not meant to be run by hand.
It answers the extension's
.BR hello ,
.BR capabilities ,
.BR create ,
.BR get ,
.B list
and
.B cancel
requests with the passkeys in the user's hirz vault.
The host validates every field again, including the origin and relying party id, builds the
WebAuthn client data itself and asks for the same approval window as
.BR hirzd (1),
showing the requesting origin too.
Without a vault on the device, the first ceremony starts the setup window,
.BR "hirz init \-\-gui" ,
under the same conditions as
.BR hirzd (1),
and a sign-in is answered as not handled at once so Firefox offers other methods.
A registration waits for the window (until the extension cancels, at most 600 seconds); when the
window leaves a vault it goes on with the normal approval, otherwise it is answered as not
handled and Firefox runs it itself.
A sign-in that finds no passkey is handed to Firefox and shows a desktop notification at most
once per site and minute.
While the vault folder reports a fork, signing in still works, registrations are answered as
not handled, and the fork is logged once on standard error.
A request that ends before any window was shown (no way to verify the user, the prompt
budget used up, zenity missing) is answered as not handled too, so a page cannot tell it from
a site hirz has no passkey for.
Requests are answered one at a time; up to 64 wait behind an open prompt, and more are
refused as busy.
.B list
and
.B capabilities
reuse the last look at the vault folder when it is less than 3 seconds old; a request that
can end in a prompt always looks first.
.PP
Messages are exchanged on standard input and output: a 32\-bit length in native byte order
followed by that many bytes of UTF\-8 JSON.
Requests are limited to 256 KiB and replies to 1 MiB.
Standard output carries only messages; diagnostics go to standard error.
.PP
The arguments Firefox passes (the path of the native manifest and the extension id) are
ignored: any local process can start the host, so they prove nothing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
when Firefox closes standard input between messages,
.B 1
on a message over the limit, a truncated message, an I/O error, or when the process cannot
harden itself or locate its folders.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HIRZ_LOG
Set to
.B debug
to log one line per message with its type, id and size, never its contents.
.TP
.B HIRZ_VAULT
Vault folder.
Default: the folder recorded in
.I vault\-path
of the state directory, else
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/hirz/vault .
.TP
.B HIRZ_STATE
Device state directory (default
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/hirz ).
.TP
.BR HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base folders; relative values are ignored.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/mozilla/native\-messaging\-hosts/hirz.json
System native messaging manifest installed by the packages (also under
.I /usr/lib64
on some distributions).
.TP
.I ~/.mozilla/native\-messaging\-hosts/hirz.json
Per\-user manifest written by
.BR "hirz setup firefox" .
It takes precedence over the system manifest; package users should not create it.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hirz/config
Approval configuration, see
.BR hirzd (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hirz (1),
.BR hirzd (1)
.PP
.I docs/extension.md
in the hirz sources.
